ce:section.ce:para:
The archaeal community structure present in reactor samples was investigated using the ANAEROCHIP microarray (Franke-Whittle et al., 2009a). Microarrays allow the determination of the presence or absence of an entire array of microorganisms in a particular sludge sample in a single experiment. Microarray data should be treated as semi-quantitative, due to the use of PCR in the amplification of environmental DNA, which can result in a disproportionate amplification of certain microorganisms, as well as the inherent differences in hybridisation efficiencies of different oligonucleotide probes (Wagner et al., 2007). The SNR values provide an estimate of the abundance of a target (Cho and Tiedje, 2002). The SNRs obtained after hybridisation of samples on the ANAEROCHIP microarray were determined and are shown in Table 2
                     , where they showed changes in archaeal communities during the experiment. Signals above the detection limit were obtained for the Methanosaeta probes Msae 841, Msae 827 and Msae 406 in the 0day samples, while two of the three Methanosaeta probes (Msae 827 and Msae 406) yielded positive signals for the day 24 and day 30 samples. Methanosaeta was virtually not detected in the day 59 and day 70 samples. In contrast, the five Methanosarcina probes (Msar 184, Msar 197, Msar 549, Msar 587 and Msar 601) gave high SNR values in the day 59 and day 70 samples, and nearly no SNR values in the earlier sludge samples. Also of interest was the disappearance of Methanoculleus over the course of the experiment. PCA of archaeal community showed the samples to be divided in two clusters, one containing samples from days 0 to 30, and the other containing samples from days 59 to 71 (Fig. 5
                     ). The strict acetotrophic genus Methanosaeta and the hydrogenotrophic genus Methanoculleus dominated samples from the first phase of the process (days 0, 24 and 30), and the facultative acetoclastic genus Methanosarcina dominated samples from the second phase of the process (sampling time 59 and 71d).
